WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.934
-You look great.
Great to see you.

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:05.534
You look stunning on the cover
of "Town &amp; Country" here too.

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:06.801
Ooh la la.
-Ooh la la.

00:00:06.801 --> 00:00:07.667
-Ooh la la, Sassoon.

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:08.968
-Ooh la la.

00:00:08.968 --> 00:00:10.000
-We have a lot to talk about,
because --

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.067
-Yeah, we do.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:12.467
-Since the last time
you were here,

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:14.267
you are now engaged
and you have a baby.

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:15.634
-Yeah, a lot of things happened.

00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:17.734
-Congratulations.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:19.067
-Oh, my God.
-I know.

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:21.100
-Are you freaking out?
How -- Are you loving it?

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.400
What's -- How's the baby?

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.434
-What if I was like,
"I -- I'm, like, 50/50"?

00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:25.601
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, yeah.

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:27.200
-No, I'm completely
obsessed with it.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.701
I love it.
Everything's different.

00:00:28.701 --> 00:00:30.133
Like, my Spotify is just like,

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.133
"Oh, you just want various
versions of 'Old MacDonald.'"

00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:33.367
That seems to be --

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:34.968
-That was your Spotify Wrapped
for the year?

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.534
-Yeah.
And my whole phone is like,

00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:38.667
"Oh, should we just
become your baby monitor?

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:40.000
Like, do you use
anything else here?"

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.534
-Yeah.
-Yeah, it's everything now.

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:42.934
I'm obsessed with him.
We're just in love.

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:44.701
-How old -- How old?
-He's, like, 14 months old.

00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:45.701
-Oh, my gosh.

00:00:45.701 --> 00:00:46.934
-I know. I know.
-Just on --

00:00:46.934 --> 00:00:48.434
I said, "like"
to be cool about it

00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:51.300
because saying "14 months
old and 2 days" isn't as cool.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.701
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah.

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:54.334
-And how is --
how are your parents?

00:00:54.334 --> 00:00:55.734
How are the grandparents?

00:00:55.734 --> 00:00:57.200
-They are obsessed.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.868
They are completely enamored.
And they're also the greatest.

00:00:59.868 --> 00:01:01.033
-They live to close
to you, right?

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:03.234
-Very, very close.
They stop by a lot.

00:01:03.234 --> 00:01:04.567
-They do?
-Yeah, a lot.

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:05.968
No, in a good way.
Sorry.

00:01:05.968 --> 00:01:07.167
-No, no, no.
-I realize, like, I'm --

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.601
-No, we didn't sense
any sarcasm there.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:09.634
-No, no. A lot.

00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:10.734
-No, yeah, of course.
You love it.

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:12.133
-It's, like,
they are wonderful.

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:13.267
-Of course, they are.
-I'm really lucky.

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:14.734
-They're the best.
-You are being sarcastic.

00:01:14.734 --> 00:01:16.467
-[ Laughs ] Am I?

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:18.133
-"They're the best."
-"Who knows?"

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:19.300
-"Who doesn't love
when parents stop by?"

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.467
-"I love when parents come by."

00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:21.667
-"I love an unannounced
stop by."

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:24.267
-"I, one day, surprise me."
Yeah, of course.

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:26.801
But is your dad --
he must be all over it.

00:01:26.801 --> 00:01:28.033
-Oh, yeah. He's so into it.

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:30.300
And also, like --
So the biggest mystery --

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.701
It's the first grandchild,

00:01:31.701 --> 00:01:33.601
and so the big question was,
like, "What are the names?"

00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:36.667
And so my parents were going for
Jay-Jay and Bub.

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:38.400
That was the goal.
My dad was gonna be Bub.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.534
But because my dad
is a little bit of a ham,

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:42.000
as you may know...
-Is he?

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.334
-Yes, slightly.

00:01:43.334 --> 00:01:44.567
And making a baby laugh

00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:46.033
is, like,
one of the greatest achievements

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:47.400
one can have in the world.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.801
-I agree.

00:01:48.801 --> 00:01:51.534
-For some reason, every time
he saw our baby, he would say --

00:01:51.534 --> 00:01:53.300
he would quote
the great Jerry Lewis,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.901
who you know as well.

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:56.334
And he'd say, "Hey, lady."

00:01:56.334 --> 00:01:58.701
But do you mind performing it
the way Jerry Lewis used to?

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:00.968
-[ As Jerry Lewis ] Hey, lady!
-Exactly.

00:02:00.968 --> 00:02:03.367
[ Laughter ]
So -- And, like --

00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:05.801
There's no, like,
logical reason.

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:07.601
-I love it!
-There's, like, a tiny baby

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:09.367
who's, like,
cute and cuddly and small

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:11.634
and, like, just learning how
to move and blink and stuff,

00:02:11.634 --> 00:02:13.934
and my dad's walking into
a quiet room and just going...

00:02:13.934 --> 00:02:17.834
-Hey, lady! Hey!
Hey, lady!

00:02:17.834 --> 00:02:20.968
-Exactly. Exactly.
-[ Normal ] Oh, my Gosh.

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:22.067
That's fantastic.

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:23.234
-His whole life,
every time he comes in,

00:02:23.234 --> 00:02:24.501
that's what he says.

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:27.334
Cut to now, guess what our son
is calling my dad?

00:02:27.334 --> 00:02:28.334
-Lady?

00:02:28.334 --> 00:02:29.334
-[ Chuckling ] Yes.

00:02:29.334 --> 00:02:32.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.601 --> 00:02:34.067
[ Applause ]

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:36.467
-That's fantastic.
You can't plan those things.

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:37.868
-No. You can't plan it.

00:02:37.868 --> 00:02:39.000
So the big question is, like,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.734
do we keep referring to him
as Bub,

00:02:40.734 --> 00:02:43.167
or do we just go with it
and say, "Lady's here"?

00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:45.033
-"Lady's here."
I love it so much.

00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:46.734
-Yeah.
-Give him my best, please.

00:02:46.734 --> 00:02:47.901
I miss him. I love him.

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:49.100
Let's talk about "M3gan."

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.200
'Cause, by the way --
-Let's talk about it.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.801
-I cannot wait for this.

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:54.434
It is the freakiest, scariest
thing ever in the world.

00:02:54.434 --> 00:02:55.567
It's getting great reviews,
by the way.

00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:58.200
95% Fresh on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.167
-Fresh.
-Fresh.

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:01.234
-Fresh. Yes.

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:02.767
-You star in it.

00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:04.667
You also executive produced it.

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:06.601
Can you set up what "M3gan"
is for everyone?

00:03:06.601 --> 00:03:12.767
-So, basically, it is
the story of a -- an A.I. robot

00:03:12.767 --> 00:03:15.767
that has been designed
to look like a doll

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:18.734
and is an ideal playmate
for a kid

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:22.367
and kind of, like,
a nanny/co-parent to a parent.

00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:23.834
-Yeah.
-And my character, Gemma,

00:03:23.834 --> 00:03:26.501
is the roboticist who is --
who has designed her.

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:29.133
And I get custody of my niece
under very tragic circumstances.

00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:31.934
And so, I think, "I don't know
what to do with a kid.

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:33.234
I know what I'll do.

00:03:33.234 --> 00:03:35.300
I'll use this robot
I've been working on

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.901
and use it to help me
raise my niece

00:03:37.901 --> 00:03:39.334
who I don't know how to handle.

00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:41.601
And I'll put them together
because the robot

00:03:41.601 --> 00:03:43.701
needs the little girl
to grow and learn.

00:03:43.701 --> 00:03:45.167
And the little girl
needs a robot to, like,

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.767
be sad about these
terrible things

00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:48.701
that have happened to her."
-Oh, my gosh.

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:52.334
-And, you know,
it just all goes really well.

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:54.501
-Yeah, of course.
Is that right? Yeah.

00:03:54.501 --> 00:03:55.901
-Yeah, yeah.
Everything's fine.

00:03:55.901 --> 00:03:57.334
And it's a 20-minute movie.

00:03:57.334 --> 00:03:59.767
-And Jason Blum?
-Yeah, Jason Blum, James Wan.

00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:01.968
We've got some, like,
horror royalty here.

00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:03.100
Did you work with them
on "Get Out"?

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.968
-I worked with Jason Blum on
"Get Out," yeah.

00:04:04.968 --> 00:04:06.033
-Whoo-hoo!
-And so did he say hey --

00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:07.300
-Yeah. Oh, thank you!
-Yeah, of course.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.567
-Thank you.
-And was he like,

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:09.901
"Hey, I have the next project
for you"?

00:04:09.901 --> 00:04:11.234
-Yeah, he literally e-mailed me,

00:04:11.234 --> 00:04:13.267
and it was like a bat signal
kind of an e-mail --

00:04:13.267 --> 00:04:14.501
one of those things
you can't really turn down,

00:04:14.501 --> 00:04:15.634
even though I didn't want to.

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:16.634
He was like, "We need you."

00:04:16.634 --> 00:04:18.267
And I was like, "Oh, God.
Okay."

00:04:18.267 --> 00:04:19.567
What am I --
-Yeah.

00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:21.767
-What do you need, another,
like, evil white girl?

00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:23.400
And he was like, "Kind of."

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.968
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.968 --> 00:04:27.167
Sort of. I have a niche.
What can I say?

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:28.667
-Yeah! Stereotype, of course.

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:29.868
-Yeah, so then I was like --

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:31.400
I didn't know anything else
about the script,

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:32.634
so I just went into it knowing

00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:35.868
just that Jason
thought I would do well in it.

00:04:35.868 --> 00:04:37.567
And so I started reading,
and then I was hooked.

00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:39.734
It's just one of those things
that is riveting.

00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:40.934
You don't know
where it's gonna go.

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:42.367
I mean, once you meet the doll,
you're like,

00:04:42.367 --> 00:04:44.033
"I don't think I'm gonna be
rooting for this doll

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:45.133
the whole time."

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.934
But in this movie,
you do root for her

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:49.167
and you're not expecting to.

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:51.300
Like, she does
some dastardly deeds,

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.133
and you're still cheering
for her to do them,

00:04:53.133 --> 00:04:54.167
which is messed up.

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:56.133
-When did you first
see the doll?

00:04:56.133 --> 00:04:57.200
-I first saw the --

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.801
I saw a bunch
of digital renderings.

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:00.968
But the first time
I actually met the doll,

00:05:00.968 --> 00:05:03.267
she was doing a camera test.

00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:04.701
And -- Which means that
before you start

00:05:04.701 --> 00:05:07.367
filming to test the lenses
and everything in wardrobe,

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:10.000
you just go into an empty
soundstage with a backdrop.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.100
Kind of, like, over there.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.734
And she was just
standing there like this.

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:14.901
And people were, like, touching
up her hair and stuff like that.

00:05:14.901 --> 00:05:16.300
And I just thought,
"This is gonna be

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.400
the scariest experience
of my life."

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.234
-What you just did was scary.

00:05:20.234 --> 00:05:21.634
-Yeah, just --
-Yeah, and that was --

00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:22.901
-And someone's just
combing her hair.

00:05:22.901 --> 00:05:23.901
And then -- Ooh.

00:05:23.901 --> 00:05:25.167
So, one of the versions
of her --

00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:26.367
We achieved her in a bunch
of different ways --

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:27.767
but one of them was animatronic.

00:05:27.767 --> 00:05:30.334
And because she blinks, her eyes
get stuck sometimes,

00:05:30.334 --> 00:05:32.000
so, they have to, like, put --

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:36.100
-One -- one lid.
-Listen, it's not --

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.133
Who says my job is easy?
-No.

00:05:38.133 --> 00:05:39.868
-You have to look --
So, there's someone

00:05:39.868 --> 00:05:43.300
putting a lubricant
on her eyeball.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:45.534
And I'm like, "She --
I know she's not real.

00:05:45.534 --> 00:05:46.801
I know she's not real."

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:48.534
But she is talking and blinking

00:05:48.534 --> 00:05:49.767
and her face is moving.
-Yeah.

00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:51.067
-And you're putting something
on her eyeball

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:52.467
and it just looks weird.

00:05:52.467 --> 00:05:53.767
-I want to show everyone what
M3gan the doll looks like.

00:05:53.767 --> 00:05:55.534
-Here's our girl.
-There she is.

00:05:55.534 --> 00:05:56.667
[ Audience murmurs ]
-Cutie.

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:58.300
-Yeah, cute.
-Now, that was a real range

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.167
of expressions.
-Yeah, emotions.

00:06:00.167 --> 00:06:01.234
Yeah. It was a roller coaster.

00:06:01.234 --> 00:06:02.567
I want to show a clip.

00:06:02.567 --> 00:06:04.033
Here's Allison Williams
in "M3gan."

00:06:04.033 --> 00:06:05.367
Take a look at this.

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:07.234
-I thought I heard something.

00:06:07.234 --> 00:06:08.801
It sounded like M3gan.

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:10.234
-If she comes in this room,

00:06:10.234 --> 00:06:12.834
I'll rip your head off right off
your neck, I swear to God.

00:06:15.801 --> 00:06:18.033
-I thought about what you said,

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:20.567
about how when
something's broken,

00:06:20.567 --> 00:06:22.100
you don't just throw it away.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:23.868
You fix it.

00:06:23.868 --> 00:06:26.501
So, that's what
I'm trying to do.

00:06:26.501 --> 00:06:29.033
But don't come in here, Cady,
it's kind of a mess.

00:06:29.033 --> 00:06:30.334
-Aunt Gemma's right, Cady.

00:06:30.334 --> 00:06:32.000
I'm all odds and ends right now.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.767
I'd really rather you didn't
see me like this.

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:35.033
-[ Gasps ]

00:06:36.267 --> 00:06:37.567
-It sounds like
you're fighting.

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:38.901
-Both: We're not fighting.

00:06:38.901 --> 00:06:41.701
-Gemma just dropped me
on the table, but I'm okay.

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:43.367
Clumsy Gemma.

00:06:43.367 --> 00:06:45.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:47.634 --> 00:06:49.133
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:49.133 --> 00:06:50.567
-What?!

00:06:50.567 --> 00:06:52.300
"Clumsy Gemma."
-"Clumsy Gemma."

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.234
-Ohh.
-Your whole physical reaction

00:06:54.234 --> 00:06:56.834
should just be the only ad
campaign we do for this movie.

00:06:56.834 --> 00:06:58.400
-That's really what I felt.
-Whatever you just did.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:59.567
-That's really what I felt.
-Yeah.

00:06:59.567 --> 00:07:01.534
-I mean, it --
when the trailer came out,

00:07:01.534 --> 00:07:04.467
it went viral immediately,
and people were freaking out.

00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:06.467
People were dressed like M3gan
for Halloween.

00:07:06.467 --> 00:07:08.100
-Before it even came out?
-Movie's not even out yet.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.467
-When we were designing her,
we were like,

00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:11.467
I mean, ideally, it's a costume
that becomes iconic

00:07:11.467 --> 00:07:13.067
and people will dress up
like her for Halloween.

00:07:13.067 --> 00:07:15.501
And it's like a cult costume
that you can identify.

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:16.901
And then last Halloween,
people were doing it.

00:07:16.901 --> 00:07:19.234
We were so excited.
It was the greatest.

00:07:19.234 --> 00:07:23.934
There's a scene where, it went
viral, of -- M3gan is dancing.

00:07:23.934 --> 00:07:25.467
-Yes.
-Down a hallway.

00:07:25.467 --> 00:07:27.567
We have that moment.
Check this out.

00:07:27.567 --> 00:07:32.934
♪♪

00:07:32.934 --> 00:07:34.734
-Yeah, yeah. So, that --
[ Audience murmurs ]

00:07:34.734 --> 00:07:37.100
That went viral. People were
posting their own versions of --

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.801
-Yep. They were setting it
to different songs.

00:07:38.801 --> 00:07:40.033
They were doing
their own versions of it.

00:07:40.033 --> 00:07:41.534
People were cartwheeling
into things.

00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:42.801
It was the greatest.
-Yeah.

00:07:42.801 --> 00:07:44.434
You guys saw that, right?
Did everyone see that?

00:07:44.434 --> 00:07:46.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:50.367 --> 00:07:53.234
[ Audience cheers ]

00:07:53.234 --> 00:07:57.133
♪♪

00:07:57.133 --> 00:08:01.133
♪♪

00:08:01.133 --> 00:08:05.033
♪♪

00:08:05.033 --> 00:08:08.968
♪♪

00:08:08.968 --> 00:08:12.868
♪♪

00:08:12.868 --> 00:08:14.968
-Oh, my gosh.

00:08:14.968 --> 00:08:17.367
Allison Williams, everyone!
"M3gan" is in theaters now.

00:08:17.367 --> 00:08:19.834
Allison and I are playing
the "Singing Whisper Challenge"

00:08:19.834 --> 00:08:20.767
when we come back.

00:08:20.767 --> 00:08:22.000
Stick around.

